  "notes": "Harmony-led girl-group pop across R&B, dancehall-pop and four-on-the-floor modes, windowed to the group's stated run. The drums fold the entry's three modes into one straight block: \"dancehall/tresillo kick patterns and R&B kick/clap with 16th hats; the pop-house records go four-on-the-floor with layered claps. Group handclaps and stomps as a signature.\" So `kick.tresilloBias` is 0.42 with `fourOnFloorInChorus` true, `hihat.base` is 16th, the clap stack is doubled with a real offset for the group-handclap layer, and the perc lanes are `clap`-adjacent (`snap`, `tom`, `rim`). The 808 is authored because the entry names it — \"808 sub with short slides in the R&B/dancehall mode; filtered pumping sub in the dance mode\" — as `counter_riff` so the pumping sub keeps generating as the bassline. Harmony is \"4-bar minor loops with 7ths\" with \"a relative-major pre-chorus\", which is why a major family sits in the set. The melody and counter blocks carry the group's defining parameter, which the entry states is arrangement rather than range: \"four-part harmony — lead plus close thirds plus an octave-doubled top, with lines redistributed each section\", so the third share is the highest in this batch, the counter runs `octave_echo` at the highest density authored here, and \"toplines trade solo lines then converge on massed harmony for hooks\" is `call_response` with a wide phrase spread. The a cappella breakdown the entry names is the deep bridge drop-out. ⚠ `confidence` is medium and the research says the parameters are \"lane-derived\": every drum probability, the humanize numbers, the harmonic weights, the melody register and `bpm.mode` 111 (the midpoint of the stated 95—128 band) are INFERRED, and the entry's [LOW-CONF] mark on the four-part-harmony voicing claim is repeated here.",
